 **Resource limits still apply.** Disk-backed processing trades RAM for temporary disk: allow substantial free space for SQLite indexes, matched-pair spill files and outputs (the exact amount depends on input size and overlap). Temporary staging files are removed after a successful run. A run killed midway can leave `pyroscope_dedupe_*` / `pyroscope_stream_*` folders in the output directory; the next `preprocess` or `harmonize` run deletes such folders when they are more than an hour old. Matching still holds one UTC day's rows for one MODIS satellite and the corresponding VIIRS time window in memory; an unusually dense single day can therefore require more RAM than an ordinary day. The default 0.5-degree analysis grid keeps the exact bootstrap's cell-level sufficient statistics bounded by the active grid cells, but very fine global grids increase that cost. `PYRO_OUT_OF_CORE=0` deliberately disables this protection.
 
 `python -m core.src.plot_overview` already scans standardized CSVs in chunks. `calendar_daily.csv` is a daily time series and remains small compared with the raw detections; downstream anomaly analysis continues to read that daily calendar.
+
+## 11. Keeping it current automatically (`auto_update`)
+
+Once section 7's one-time archive backfill and `train_countries` have run, `auto_update` keeps the calendar and the forecast fresh without re-running the whole pipeline by hand:
+
+```bash
+python manage.py auto_update
+```
+
+On its first run in a UTC day it (1) looks at the newest day already in `calendar_daily.csv`, (2) downloads NASA FIRMS **NRT** data (`core.src.download_firms`, not the ~5-month-delayed `_SP` archive sources) for every day after that through today UTC, (3) re-runs `preprocess` + `harmonize`, and (4) re-runs `predict_countries` so `forecast_by_country.csv` reflects the new data. Re-running it again the same UTC day is a no-op (tracked in `data/processed/auto_update_state.json`); pass `--force` to pull again anyway, which also re-downloads today's NRT file since NRT data streams in throughout the day. If `calendar_daily.csv` does not exist yet it fetches `--initial-lookback-days` (default 10) of NRT data instead of failing, but that is only enough for a demo -- do the real archive backfill for anything else. If no trained model is found at `data/outputs/model_countries.joblib` the calendar is still refreshed but `predict_countries` is skipped with a message to run `train_countries` first.
+
+Options: `--sources` (default `MODIS_NRT VIIRS_SNPP_NRT`; an `_SP` name is mapped to its `_NRT` equivalent automatically), `--bbox` (default `$PYRO_BBOX`, else global), `--skip-predict` (refresh the calendar only), `--with-anomalies` (also run `detect_anomalies`). Schedule it once a day with cron / Windows Task Scheduler / a CI schedule; it is always safe to run more often by hand, since the state file is what limits it to one real pull per UTC day.
